course: dictionary
lesson: 3140
title: Eloquent: saying it without saying it
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: Eloquent means able to speak or write well, so clearly and movingly that people are persuaded or touched. Look for it when someone is praised for their words. «You're so eloquent, dear Badger» is Toad praising the way Badger talks, so it must mean good with words.
explain: The clues are in the surrounding words. Toad is talking about speaking and persuading, so eloquent fits with words, not with looks. Always try your meaning in the sentence: "You're so good with words, dear Badger" still makes sense, so you have it right.
explain: Eloquent can also describe a silence, a look or a gesture that shows a feeling clearly without words. An eloquent silence tells you someone is cross, sad or sorry, even though they say nothing. Ask yourself: is the feeling clear to me, even though no one speaks?
fact: Eloquent is an adjective: eloquent, more eloquent, most eloquent.
fact: «Eloquent» means able to speak or write well, clearly and movingly.
fact: Eloquent speeches, speakers and writers are praised in English and history lessons.
fact: «Eloquent» can also describe a silence, a look or a gesture that shows a feeling without words, such as «an eloquent silence».
fact: Check the sense by swapping in a simpler idea: "good with words" for speakers, "showing a feeling clearly" for silences and looks.
exercise: «You're so eloquent, dear Badger.» What does eloquent mean here? | *Able to speak well and persuade | Very tired and sleepy | Quiet and hidden | Cross and grumpy
exercise: Maya says, "Her silence was eloquent." What does this suggest? | She was fast asleep | She had lost her voice forever | Her silence was rude and loud | *Her silence showed a feeling clearly, without words
exercise: Find and copy the word that describes a speaker who speaks well: "The eloquent speaker won over the whole class." | whole | speaker | class | *eloquent
test: A teacher praises a student's eloquent essay. Which meaning fits best? | Badly spelled and hard to read | *Well written and clearly moving | Covered in ink and messy | Very short and quick
test: Which word means about the same as eloquent in sense 1? | Loud and rude | Silent and shy | Tired and slow | *Articulate and persuasive
exam: Which sentence uses eloquent to mean showing a feeling without words? | *Her eloquent look told me she was upset, though she said nothing | The eloquent speaker thanked the crowd | The eloquent writer finished her book | Nadia was eloquent in the maths test
